[TestRuleVMAlertCheck/latency_offset_without_eval_delay - 1]
- description: latency_offset without eval_delay
  content: |
    - name: g
      params:
        latency_offset: ["3m"]
      rules:
      - alert: Down
        expr: up == 0
  output: |
    3 |     latency_offset: ["3m"]
                              ^^
                              VictoriaMetrics answers queries of this group with data at least 3m old, but
                              vmalert evaluates them 30s before now, `-rule.evalDelay`, 30s by default, as
                              the group doesn't set `eval_delay`, and stamps the results with that time, so
                              alerts and recording rule results lag 2m30s behind. Set `eval_delay: 3m`.
  problem:
    reporter: rule/vmalert
    summary: latency_offset longer than eval_delay
    details: '`latency_offset` in group `params` overrides `-search.latencyOffset`: VictoriaMetrics doesn''t return samples newer than that and moves the time of a query back to `now - latency_offset` when it''s newer. vmalert evaluates rules `eval_delay` before now, `-rule.evalDelay` (30s by default) when the group doesn''t set it, and stamps the results with that time. vmalert docs recommend setting `eval_delay` equal to `latency_offset`, see [eval_delay](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/vmalert/#groups) and [query latency](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/keyconcepts/#query-latency). If vmalert runs with a `-rule.evalDelay` longer than the default, set `eval_delay` in the group anyway.'
    diagnostics:
        - message: 'VictoriaMetrics answers queries of this group with data at least 3m old, but vmalert evaluates them 30s before now, `-rule.evalDelay`, 30s by default, as the group doesn''t set `eval_delay`, and stamps the results with that time, so alerts and recording rule results lag 2m30s behind. Set `eval_delay: 3m`.'
          firstcolumn: 1
          lastcolumn: 2
          kind: 0
    lines:
        first: 3
        last: 3
    severity: 1
    anchor: 0

